<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Why each of these three keys is here, and what actually constrains the
	broad one.

	com.apple.security.app-sandbox
	  An App Extension must be sandboxed or PlugInKit will not load it. This
	  is also what makes the temporary exception below mean anything at all:
	  a `temporary-exception` entitlement is a *relaxation of the sandbox*
	  and is silently inert in a process that is not sandboxed.

	com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only
	  QuickLook hands the extension a sandbox extension ("consumable token")
	  for the previewed document. Consuming it requires this entitlement, so
	  without it the extension cannot read the very file it was asked to
	  preview.

	com.apple.security.network.client
	  The preview is a WKWebView showing the document's rendered HTML, and a
	  markdown file may reference things that live off the machine: an
	  ![](https://...) image, a YouTube or other oembed iframe, a remote
	  stylesheet. Outbound connections from a sandboxed process are refused
	  without this, so those would silently render as broken images and empty
	  frames.

	  Note this is NOT in tension with the "QuickLook has no network"
	  invariant in src/quicklook.rs. That one is narrower and still holds: it
	  says *mbr's own* assets must be inlined rather than fetched (which is
	  why the highlight.js grammar is embedded instead of pulled from a CDN),
	  so a preview always renders completely with no network at all. This
	  entitlement is about the *document's* content, which mbr does not
	  control and cannot inline.

	All three were present when this file was added (8be4ef8) and were dropped
	by 430a3d2 ("Quicklook improvements"), whose diff rewrote the file's
	indentation from spaces to tabs and lost them along the way; the commit
	message mentions no entitlement change, so the removal was collateral
	rather than intended.

	Under Xcode these keys are easy to lose without noticing, because Xcode
	injects its own (app-sandbox, get-task-allow, ...) into a Debug build's
	signature regardless of this file. The Nix build signs with exactly what
	is written here and nothing else, so this file is the only thing standing
	between the shipped extension and a sandbox it cannot read files in.

	--- on the breadth of the temporary exception ---

	QuickLook hands the extension a sandbox extension for the user-selected
	document only. Everything else a preview needs - sibling images, videos and
	PDFs referenced from the markdown, plus .mbr/theme.css, .mbr/user.css and
	.mbr/config.toml at the repository root - is not covered by that grant, and
	there is no entitlement scoped to "the folder this document lives in".

	The narrower alternative, com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files
	.home-relative-path.read-only = "/", would cover repositories under $HOME but
	silently break assets and custom themes for repositories on external volumes
	(/Volumes/...) or anywhere else off the home directory. mbr's premise is that
	it works against any markdown repository, so that trade was not taken.

	The real constraint is therefore enforced in code, not by this file:
	MBRFileSchemeHandler (PreviewViewController.swift) serves an mbrfile:// URL
	only when the requested path, fully resolved with realpath(3), lies inside
	the previewed document's repository root, and refuses everything until that
	root is established. Without that check this entitlement lets a <script> in
	an untrusted .md read any file the user can read.

	If this grant is ever widened to read-write, or the handler's containment
	check is removed, revisit both halves together.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-only</key>
	<array>
		<string>/</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
